    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d867">

                <pb facs="0664" n="1864" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v07p2/pg1864"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">663.001/5–1653: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 867<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the
                        Embassy in the United Kingdom</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d867-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-05-16T14:56:00-04:00">May 16, 1953—2:56
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7439. For Dowling only. Deptel 7438<anchor n="2" xml:id="d867-f2"/> drafted in
                    terms to permit your showing to your French and UK colleagues. For this reason
                    we did not mention authority which we have to sign long treaty as is if changes
                    impossible and in order prevent break-up of meeting with onus on West. Therefore
                    should Soviets at any time make it unmistakably clear that they will break up
                    meeting unless we agree to discuss long treaty without reopening agreed articles
                    you <pb facs="0665" n="1865" xml:id="pg1865"/> are authorized to negotiate with
                    view obtaining what you can on unagreed articles.</p>
                <p>You are authorized to act likewise in unlikely case Soviets at any time should
                    move immediate conclusion of long draft treaty.</p>
                <p>In both cases preferable, if you think time available, to consult Department but
                    this should not be done at serious risk of Soviet walk-out.</p>
                <p>While it is obvious that our negotiating latitude may be largely conditioned by
                    Soviet tactics and while this telegram is intended give you that latitude which
                    may be necessary to prevent break-up of meeting with West bearing onus, bear in
                    mind Secretary’s instruction that we make wholehearted effort in support of
                    short draft and should these efforts be unsuccessful that we make determined
                    effort to amend Article 35.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d867-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d867-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted by Knight and cleared with Bonbright. According to notations on a
                    memorandum by Bonbright to Smith, May 18, the Acting Secretary did not approve
                    the text of this telegram until 2 days after it was transmitted.
                    (663.001/5–1653)</p>
            </note>
